-- An #ASSERT whose expression RAISES is neither satisfied nor failed: the
-- evaluator could not decide it, and the report must say so — and say why.
-- Before this was pinned, both polarities of a raising assertion reported
-- "assertion failed", so a wrong answer and an error were indistinguishable.

ASSUME x IS A NUMBER
ASSUME b IS A BOOLEAN

-- raises: division by zero — both polarities "could not be evaluated"
#ASSERT (1 DIVIDED BY 0) EQUALS 1
#ASSERT NOT ((1 DIVIDED BY 0) EQUALS 1)

-- raises: stuck on an assumed term — both polarities "could not be evaluated"
#ASSERT x EQUALS 1
#ASSERT NOT (x EQUALS 1)

-- does not raise: the disjunction short-circuits before x is forced
#ASSERT TRUE OR (x EQUALS 1)

-- a bare assumed BOOLEAN is no verdict either — neither TRUE nor FALSE.
-- `#ASSERT NOT b` forces b and raises; `#ASSERT b` reduces to the symbolic b
-- WITHOUT raising, and used to fall through to "assertion failed", so the
-- two polarities disagreed. Both "could not be evaluated".
#ASSERT b
#ASSERT NOT b

-- the two clean verdicts, for contrast
#ASSERT 1 EQUALS 2
#ASSERT 1 EQUALS 1
